%I A400352 #17 Sep 30 2026 22:47:57
%S A400352 4,11,17,103,733,27539,13856993
%N A400352 Least integer m > 1 that does not occur as a denominator in any representation of 1 as a sum of n distinct unit fractions.
%C A400352 a(9) = 13856993 was proved, every m < 13856993 has 9 term representation, verified with arithmetic (some found by GitHub user: ntysdd), with an exhaustive search showing none exist for 13856993. Note that every term after a(3) is prime.
%D A400352 P. Erdős and R. L. Graham, Old and New Problems and Results in Combinatorial Number Theory, Monographies de L'Enseignement Mathématique 28, Geneva, 1980, p. 35.
%D A400352 W. van Doorn and Q. Tang, The smallest denominator not contained in a unit fraction decomposition of 1 with fixed length, Math. Proc. Cambridge Philos. Soc. (2026).
%H A400352 T. F. Bloom, <a href="https://www.erdosproblems.com/293">Problem 293</a>, Erdős Problems.
%H A400352 Muhamadiev Faridun, <a href="https://gitlab.com/faridunislom/erdos293">Computation of a(8)</a>, 2026.
%H A400352 Wouter van Doorn and Quanyu Tang, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.22083">The smallest denominator not contained in a unit fraction decomposition of 1 with fixed length</a>, arXiv:2512.22083 [math.NT], 2025.
%H A400352 Jude Wallis, <a href="https://github.com/EconLearn/erdos293-check">Certificates and exhaustive search for a(9)</a>, 2026.
%e A400352 a(3) = 4: the only way to write 1 as a sum of 3 distinct unit fractions is 1 = 1/2 + 1/3 + 1/6, so 2 and 3 occur as denominators but 4 does not.
%e A400352 a(4) = 11: the six 4-term representations have denominators {2,3,7,42}, {2,3,8,24}, {2,3,9,18}, {2,3,10,15}, {2,4,5,20}, {2,4,6,12}. So every integer from 2 to 10 occurs but 11 doesn't.
%Y A400352 Cf. A000058, A006585, A097048, A097049
%K A400352 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A400352 3,1
%A A400352 _Jude Wallis_, Sep 23 2026
